.th LOG I 9/9/77
.sh NAME
log \*- file exchange package for remote unices
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd log
remote
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Log
enables you to talk to another operating system
via a remote file
(\ sometimes a dial up tty line\ )
in an almost transparent fashion.
The exception is the local quit code
which opens the door to
.it log'\c
s more interesting features.

.it Log
has 2 modes: ``tty'' & ``file''.

After invoking
.it log
you are in transparent ``tty'' mode
talking to the remote file.

The quit code
reported at the start of ``tty'' mode
returns your tty to ``file'' mode,
and if, hopefully, you have been talking to another UNIX system,
you may now exchange files with it.
(\ 
.it log
will invoke an image of itself to talk to on the other system\ ).
.it Log
now uses a moderately sophisticated file exchange protocol for
transferring binary files with full crc checks, timeouts, etc.
The number of bytes transferred is reported at the end of each transfer.

The file exchange syntax is demonstrated by the null command
( just type <cr> ).
There is a facility for local escape to the shell,
and for changing the quit code.
Also, in this mode, DEL will get you out of
.it log.
.sh "SEE ALSO"
.it utalk
(I) to talk to less intelligent systems
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
.it Log
may report protocol errors.
Too many mean you have a bad line
(\ which will slow up file transfer\ ).
.sh AUTHOR
Ian Johnstone
( University of New South Wales )
.br
Piers Lauder
